steps.body:
The last lesson kept to ASCII, where every character is one byte. Go source and most text is UTF-8, where a character like `é` takes two bytes. So `len(s)` still counts **bytes**, not characters.

There are two ways to loop, and they visit different things:

```go
for i := 0; i < len(s); i++ {
	fmt.Print(s[i]) // a byte (uint8), printed as a number
}
for i, r := range s {
	fmt.Printf("%d:%c ", i, r) // a rune (int32) and its byte offset
}
```

The index loop hands you raw bytes. `range` decodes UTF-8 for you: `r` is a whole **rune**, an `int32` code point, and `i` is the byte offset where it starts, so offsets can jump by 2 or more. Print a rune with `%c` to see the character instead of its number.

To count characters, use `utf8.RuneCountInString(s)` from `import "unicode/utf8"` (you call it with the last part of the path, `utf8`), or `len([]rune(s))`.

Gotcha: indexing grabs one byte, not one character. On `"café"`, `s[3]` is 195, the first of the two bytes of `é`, and `string(s[3])` turns 195 into the character with that code point, so it prints `Ã`.

example.code:
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"unicode/utf8"
)

func main() {
	s := "café"
	fmt.Println(len(s), utf8.RuneCountInString(s))
	fmt.Printf("%T %T\n", s[0], 'é')

	for i := 0; i < len(s); i++ {
		if i > 0 {
			fmt.Print(" ")
		}
		fmt.Print(s[i])
	}
	fmt.Println()

	for i, r := range s {
		if i > 0 {
			fmt.Print(" ")
		}
		fmt.Printf("%d:%c", i, r)
	}
	fmt.Println()
}

